  <h2>Tips for making this plan work</h2>

  <p><strong>Batch cook on Sunday.</strong> Make the egg muffin cups, overnight oats, and slow cooker chicken all on Sunday. That gives you ready-made meals for several days with minimal weekday effort.</p>

  <p><strong>Swap freely.</strong> Don't like fish? Swap salmon or cod for an extra chicken meal. Vegetarian some days? Swap the protein for a tin of chickpeas or extra eggs. The structure matters more than the specific recipes.</p>

  <p><strong>Skip the snack if you're not hungry.</strong> The snacks are optional — they're there to bring you closer to 1,200 calories if your three meals fall short. Don't force them.</p>

  <p><strong>Adjust portions to your appetite.</strong> Some days you'll eat closer to 1,500 calories, others closer to 1,000. That's normal on GLP-1 medications. Aim for the protein target first, then fill in with healthy carbs and fats as appetite allows.</p>

  <p><strong>Keep emergency protein on hand.</strong> String cheese, Greek yoghurt cups, protein bars, and pre-cooked chicken strips are all useful for days when cooking feels impossible. Having something ready means you're less likely to skip meals entirely.</p>

  <p><em>Calorie and protein counts are approximate and will vary based on exact brands and portion sizes. This meal plan is for general informational purposes and is not a substitute for personalised dietary advice from a registered dietitian or your healthcare provider.</em></p>

